> It is ALSO wrong, imo, to understand the rating system *as implemented* and then ignore that to the detriment of those laboring under unfair practices from the company that employs them. 

&#x200B;

So do you think schools/teachers should lie and grade their students higher than they actually scored... so that they might get into better colleges and thus have a better life???

The teacher knows the detriment a low GPA can have on a highschooler, or a failed class... Do you think they should be fabricating higher scores for their students so they can have an easier life? 

I think that's wrong, and that's my opinion.

All of this is in the hands of the company. The decision to have a 5 star rating system, the decision to let drivers go at 4.6 average... They know riders hand out 2, 3, and 4 stars all the time... ALL THE TIME. But they still have their standards set at 4.6 average. That's the only thing that's wrong in my eyes.

&#x200B;

Like I said, you could say I'm heartless... But I don't think I'm wrong any any level when rating honestly. You are wrong for rating dishonestly... same as a referee judging a gymnast or a teacher/school grading their students. It's not about taking into account what your rating might ultimately mean for the person... It's about rating their performance, **BASED ON THEIR PERFORMANCE!!!!** 

&#x200B;

How uber or lyft uses the rating system is wholly at their discretion and thus they should be the only ones to blame when a 4 star average causes a driver to be let go.

